{
  "id": "zero_sum.lam_mu_existence",
  "title": "Existence of Optimal Mixed Strategies (Loomis Foundations)",
  "kind": "theorem",
  "status": "formalized",
  "href": "zero_sum/zero_sum_lam_mu_existence.html",
  "primary_topic": "zero_sum",
  "topics": [
    "zero_sum",
    "zero_sum.minimax"
  ],
  "body_html": "<h1>Existence of Optimal Mixed Strategies (Loomis Foundations)</h1>\n<p>For a finite matrix game $A : I \\times J \\to \\mathbb{R}$, the row player's\nguarantee function is\n$$\\lambda_{\\mathrm{aux}}(x) = \\min_{j \\in J} \\sum_{i \\in I} x_i A(i,j),$$\nand the column player's loss-cap function is\n$$\\mu_{\\mathrm{aux}}(y) = \\max_{i \\in I} \\sum_{j \\in J} y_j A(i,j).$$\nThe two scalar values\n$$\\lambda_0 = \\sup_{x \\in \\Delta(I)} \\lambda_{\\mathrm{aux}}(x) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad\n  \\mu_0 = \\inf_{y \\in \\Delta(J)} \\mu_{\\mathrm{aux}}(y)$$\nare the maxmin and minmax values of the game.</p>\n<p>This node packages four facts that hold <em>before</em> the inductive minimax\nidentity is established:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Continuity.</strong> Both $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{aux}}$ and $\\mu_{\\mathrm{aux}}$ are\n   continuous on the (compact) mixed-strategy simplices.</li>\n<li><strong>Boundedness.</strong> $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{aux}}$ is bounded above and\n   $\\mu_{\\mathrm{aux}}$ is bounded below by the obvious extremes of $A$.</li>\n<li><strong>Achievement.</strong> There exist mixed strategies $x^\\ast$ and $y^\\ast$ such\n   that $\\sum_i x^\\ast_i A(i,j) \\ge \\lambda_0$ for every column $j$ and\n   $\\sum_j y^\\ast_j A(i,j) \\le \\mu_0$ for every row $i$.</li>\n<li><strong>Weak duality.</strong> $\\lambda_0 \\le \\mu_0$.</li>\n</ol>",
  "proof_html": "<p>Continuity of $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{aux}}$ is the finite infimum of\ncontinuous weighted sums (<code>Continuous.finset_inf'_apply</code>); the proof for\n$\\mu_{\\mathrm{aux}}$ is dual. Boundedness follows because each weighted sum\nis bounded by the extreme matrix entry. The supremum\n$\\lambda_0$ is achieved by the extreme-value theorem applied to the compact\nmixed-strategy simplex (<code>IsCompact.exists_isMaxOn</code>), and the optimiser\nrealises $\\lambda_0$ on every pure column by the infimum definition; the\n$\\mu_0$ side is symmetric. Weak duality combines the two optimisers in the\nbilinear payoff:\n$$\\lambda_0 \\le \\sum_{i,j} x^\\ast_i y^\\ast_j A(i,j) \\le \\mu_0,$$\nusing the pointwise-to-weighted bound transfer from\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.loomis_route_compactness\" href=\"zero_sum_loomis_route_compactness.html\">Compactness and Continuity Scaffold for the Loomis Route</a>.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Chapter 2, Section 2.3] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Existence of mixed optimisers and weak duality, prior to the inductive minimax theorem.</li>\n</ul>",
